# Trivy ignore file — documented, time-bounded suppressions.
# Each entry records WHY a finding is accepted and the trigger to revisit it.
# Reviewed every release; remove entries the moment the upstream gate clears.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# starlette 1.x advisories — RESOLVED 2026-08-15, suppressions REMOVED.
#
# These entries (CVE-2026-48818, CVE-2026-54283) were held because the fixed
# starlette required fastapi >= 0.137, whose internal `_IncludedRouter` route
# type broke prometheus-fastapi-instrumentator. That revisit trigger has since
# fired and nobody deleted the entries: the project now ships fastapi 0.138.2,
# starlette 1.3.1 and instrumentator 8.0.2 (see backend/poetry.lock).
#
# Verified 2026-08-15: `pip-audit` against the current lock with NO --ignore-vuln
# flags reports "No known vulnerabilities found". Keeping the suppressions would
# have silently blinded the gate to any FUTURE advisory carrying these IDs.
#
# Lesson worth keeping: a time-bounded suppression only works if someone checks
# the trigger. Re-run pip-audit with no ignores before adding another one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# caddy edge binary — Go stdlib advisory, UPSTREAM-GATED (cannot remediate yet).
#
# CVE-2026-42504 — net/textproto MIME header decode (DoS) in the Go stdlib that
# the official caddy:2.11.4-alpine image is compiled with (Go 1.26.3; fixed in
# Go 1.26.4). 2.11.4 is the latest caddy release, so the bundled toolchain only
# advances when caddy publishes a rebuild. The edge image already runs
# `apk upgrade` for OS packages (openssl, etc.); the caddy binary itself is the
# remaining gap. Exposure is limited: the edge terminates TLS and reverse-proxies
# trusted internal services; it is not a general-purpose mail/MIME processor.
#
# Revisit trigger: caddy ships a release built with Go >= 1.26.4 -> bump the
# `caddy:` base tag in frontend/Dockerfile and delete this line.
CVE-2026-42504
